of things to think about is very much the problem,

00:15:17.380 --> 00:15:19.500
the business case and the rulebook.

00:15:19.500 --> 00:15:22.260
Only then do you know what in the heck you're building.

00:15:22.260 --> 00:15:25.260
So as I was saying, there's many, many different ways

00:15:25.260 --> 00:15:27.500
to implement a dataspace technically

00:15:28.900 --> 00:15:32.420
while being confined by some standards.

00:15:32.420 --> 00:15:37.420
But yeah, it's important to just reiterate

00:15:38.380 --> 00:15:40.500
that yet again, that it's really important

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:41.720
to start with a problem.

00:15:42.720 --> 00:15:45.340
And aggregators can play a role in a dataspace.

00:15:45.340 --> 00:15:46.740
So it doesn't mean you have to exclude

00:15:46.740 --> 00:15:49.020
all data aggregators from dataspaces,

00:15:49.020 --> 00:15:53.620
but yeah, generally it's a federated system

00:15:53.620 --> 00:15:58.620
among operational databases around the place.

00:16:02.240 --> 00:16:05.560
And I'm flogging this a lot,

00:16:05.560 --> 00:16:08.380
but it's just because it's come up so many times

00:16:08.380 --> 00:16:10.440
as a misunderstanding this difference

00:16:10.480 --> 00:16:13.000
between the control plane and the data plane,

00:16:13.000 --> 00:16:14.320
that the control plane,

00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:19.400
it's where you're articulating how the rules

00:16:19.400 --> 00:16:24.400
of exchange happen, how the usage is managed,

00:16:26.200 --> 00:16:30.560
how you check that everybody has their correct attributes

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:31.760
to access the data.

00:16:32.720 --> 00:16:34.440
A lot of standards involved in that

00:16:34.440 --> 00:16:37.360
and we'll get into that a little bit more later.

00:16:37.360 --> 00:16:40.640
Whereas the data plane can be absolutely

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:43.280
almost anything you can imagine,

00:16:43.280 --> 00:16:45.260
but almost anything you imagine

00:16:45.260 --> 00:16:48.960
is not necessarily implemented in an open source solution.

00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:50.600
So you might have to build it yourself.

00:16:50.600 --> 00:16:53.480
So if you're imagining something really cool,

00:16:53.480 --> 00:16:54.880
you might have to build it.

00:16:56.240 --> 00:16:58.980
It might not be an off the box solution

00:16:58.980 --> 00:17:04.600
that you can get from Eclipse.

00:17:04.600 --> 00:17:07.000
So there was also some question around

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:13.640
what's the difference between a rulebook and a connector?

00:17:13.640 --> 00:17:16.240
Like when is the connector making decisions

00:17:16.240 --> 00:17:18.640
or that kind of thinking?

00:17:19.520 --> 00:17:20.360
And it really is,

00:17:20.360 --> 00:17:24.380
the rulebook is all about the community deciding the rules,

00:17:24.380 --> 00:17:29.380
who can join, who's allowed to access the data,

00:17:29.440 --> 00:17:32.760
who is verifying the various trust tokens

00:17:32.760 --> 00:17:34.260
that need to be verified.

00:17:35.680 --> 00:17:40.480
Have we got legal sign off and policy sign off?

00:17:40.480 --> 00:17:42.880
How are we funding all of this?

00:17:42.880 --> 00:17:44.320
How are these disputes,

00:17:44.320 --> 00:17:47.300
any disputes to a core data exchange?

00:17:47.300 --> 00:17:48.920
How are those going to be handled?

00:17:50.540 --> 00:17:55.460
So all of that kind of thing is what the rulebook decides.

00:17:55.460 --> 00:17:59.600
And then the technical architecture is there

00:17:59.600 --> 00:18:02.680
to be able to work with whatever is decided

00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:07.880
to build a connector that enforces all of that.

00:18:07.880 --> 00:18:10.480
And we'll talk a little bit more about how that's done

00:18:10.480 --> 00:18:11.780
at the connector level.

00:18:13.860 --> 00:18:18.860
But it's a bit of work to get everything machine

00:18:20.320 --> 00:18:23.000
actionable and readable, right?

00:18:23.000 --> 00:18:25.100
From a policy standpoint,

00:18:25.100 --> 00:18:26.100
but once it's done,

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:29.520
it really does facilitate doing this rapidly at scale

00:18:29.520 --> 00:18:33.280
and the data does start to flow.

00:18:33.280 --> 00:18:37.200
And so the bridge between the rulebook and the connector

00:18:37.200 --> 00:18:41.560
is so you have this Open Digital Rights Language (ODRL), right?

00:18:41.560 --> 00:18:46.560
This ODRL and it's just a common framework

00:18:48.720 --> 00:18:53.720
that W3C has come up with or how we specify licenses

00:18:56.040 --> 00:18:57.820
and use and all kinds of things, right?

00:18:57.820 --> 00:19:02.040
So it's just a standard for the way we give data

00:19:02.040 --> 00:19:03.280
around a policy.

00:19:04.280 --> 00:19:08.280
But what the connector does is it recognizes that

00:19:11.360 --> 00:19:14.560
ODRL language and runs a function

00:19:14.560 --> 00:19:16.880
and different connectors will run that function

00:19:16.880 --> 00:19:17.720
in different ways,

00:19:17.720 --> 00:19:22.720
but that function is actually implementing the policy piece.

00:19:23.600 --> 00:19:28.200
And writing that bit of that function is not an easy thing.

00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:31.360
And that's why it's nice that there's 18 to 23

00:19:31.520 --> 00:19:36.080
of these policy classes already available off the shelf.

00:19:36.080 --> 00:19:38.160
So because building one is not easy

00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:40.320
and building one that works well and is secure

00:19:40.320 --> 00:19:42.220
and all the rest of it is not easy.

00:19:42.220 --> 00:19:47.790
And we'll come back to that a little bit later as well.

00:19:47.790 --> 00:19:54.100
So one of the key things that I wanted to highlight today

00:19:55.100 --> 00:20:00.100
that is the secret sauce to make dataspaces interoperable.

00:20:00.260 --> 00:20:04.260
So when dataspaces were first started over 10 years ago,

00:20:04.260 --> 00:20:07.970
all these cool dataspaces were built,

00:20:07.970 --> 00:20:12.970
but when they went to try and push and pull data

00:20:12.970 --> 00:20:15.330
between dataspaces, it didn't work.

00:20:16.330 --> 00:20:21.330
And so they came up with, I think about five years ago,

00:20:21.730 --> 00:20:24.970
started coming up with this dataspace protocol,

00:20:24.970 --> 00:20:28.970
which is a way to ensure that all the schemas

00:20:29.210 --> 00:20:34.210
and all the machine language that drives these things

00:20:35.170 --> 00:20:39.730
is recognized by other machines, essentially, right?

00:20:39.730 --> 00:20:44.730
So if a connector is not set up to recognize the version

00:20:47.410 --> 00:20:50.890
of the DSP that is being run from another connector,

00:20:50.890 --> 00:20:53.090
they can't talk to each other, right?

00:20:53.090 --> 00:20:58.090
So the DSP, so your connector can grow

00:20:58.970 --> 00:21:03.290
on the number of versions of the DSP that it operates on.

00:21:03.290 --> 00:21:04.250
So when you first set it,

00:21:04.250 --> 00:21:07.130
it will just set the most recent version of the DSP,

00:21:07.130 --> 00:21:11.530
but you might need to update as updates come,

00:21:11.530 --> 00:21:16.620
this dataspace protocol.

00:21:16.620 --> 00:21:19.100
And it's easy to miss because when it's working,

00:21:19.100 --> 00:21:21.500
like it's not something that people talk about.

00:21:21.500 --> 00:21:25.300
It's very much like data standards for ordinary databases.

00:21:25.300 --> 00:21:28.180
It's not something that people spend a lot of time in,

00:21:28.340 --> 00:21:32.380
but it is the magic sauce that allows two connectors

00:21:32.380 --> 00:21:33.660
from different dataspaces

00:21:33.660 --> 00:21:37.520
to understand what each other are saying.

00:21:37.520 --> 00:21:41.920
And so one of the first parts of a dataspace protocol

00:21:41.920 --> 00:21:46.920
is being able to make your data easy to discover

00:21:48.960 --> 00:21:51.520
and understand what's available, right?

00:21:51.520 --> 00:21:55.680
So DCAT is a standard, again,

00:21:55.680 --> 00:22:00.120
by the World Wide Web Consortium, W3C,

00:22:01.360 --> 00:22:05.720
that when you put your data in that standard,

00:22:07.280 --> 00:22:08.680
tons more people can read it.

00:22:08.680 --> 00:22:12.200
So that's what the W3C is all about,

00:22:12.200 --> 00:22:15.280
is it's making standards that are interoperable

00:22:15.280 --> 00:22:16.480
across the web, right?

00:22:17.400 --> 00:22:22.400
And so it allows any catalog to,

00:22:23.160 --> 00:22:25.240
or anybody to visit a catalog

00:22:25.240 --> 00:22:27.000
who's allowed to visit the catalog,

00:22:27.000 --> 00:22:29.760
keep in mind some catalogs have access control.

00:22:30.680 --> 00:22:33.480
So they might only let a few people access their catalog,

00:22:34.400 --> 00:22:38.440
but the idea is that you're making this easy to find

00:22:38.440 --> 00:22:41.880
and look at from all parties.

00:22:41.880 --> 00:22:44.760
So that standard's important

00:22:44.760 --> 00:22:48.520
and the provider will publish their data in that format

00:22:49.440 --> 00:22:54.200
and include the policy that governs use

00:22:54.200 --> 00:23:00.480
within that DCAT catalog.

00:23:00.520 --> 00:23:02.320
And again, catalogs can be federated

00:23:02.320 --> 00:23:03.680
or they can be centralized.

00:23:03.680 --> 00:23:06.920
There's all kinds of different ways that you can do this,

00:23:06.920 --> 00:23:10.760
but you do ideally need to follow this standard

00:23:10.760 --> 00:23:14.760
for how you present your metadata

00:23:14.760 --> 00:23:19.720
from your database that you're sharing.

00:23:19.720 --> 00:23:23.840
And so the data consumer then is gonna be able

00:23:23.840 --> 00:23:27.920
to request data from that catalog.

00:23:27.920 --> 00:23:32.080
So that's where the jumping off point happens.

00:23:32.080 --> 00:23:35.080
It's not a centralized database, right?

00:23:35.080 --> 00:23:36.360
You're going to a catalog

00:23:36.360 --> 00:23:38.440
and then you're probably gonna have to provide

00:23:38.440 --> 00:23:41.440
a whole bunch of tokens to prove that you are

00:23:41.440 --> 00:23:42.840
who you say you are

00:23:42.840 --> 00:23:45.600
and you have all the right attributes

00:23:45.600 --> 00:23:48.120
to be able to even request data in the first place.

00:23:48.120 --> 00:23:49.640
So a lot of people put this

00:23:49.640 --> 00:23:52.320
at the front end of the dataspace

00:23:52.320 --> 00:23:57.320
to sort of allow for automation of this next step.

00:23:58.800 --> 00:24:01.240
And this next step, again, it can be kind of manual,

00:24:01.240 --> 00:24:04.640
which is what the RMIT testbed is showing

00:24:04.640 --> 00:24:06.160
or it can be all automated,

00:24:07.000 --> 00:24:10.480
but the machines need to know

00:24:10.480 --> 00:24:13.160
how we're going to agree on a contract.

00:24:16.680 --> 00:24:18.680
And this is the usage contract.

00:24:18.680 --> 00:24:21.640
So how are we going to agree on,

00:24:21.640 --> 00:24:24.920
first, I'm gonna request data.

00:24:24.920 --> 00:24:27.440
I'm gonna offer, make an offer to that person

00:24:27.440 --> 00:24:28.400
who made the request

00:24:28.400 --> 00:24:32.320
and it's gonna include all of our conditions.

00:24:32.320 --> 00:24:36.440
Then that person is going to accept it either manually

00:24:36.440 --> 00:24:38.040
or this is gonna happen automatically,

00:24:38.040 --> 00:24:40.680
again, depending on how you've treated the front end.

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:43.760
Then it's agreed and it's verified.

00:24:43.760 --> 00:24:46.480
So there's all this tic-tac back and forth that says,

00:24:46.480 --> 00:24:48.960
yeah, okay, we've agreed to these terms.

00:24:48.960 --> 00:24:53.960
Often those agreements are pushed into logs

00:24:54.560 --> 00:24:58.600
that are then reviewed by independent people,

00:24:58.600 --> 00:25:01.840
maybe auditors or others who are administering the dataspace

00:25:01.840 --> 00:25:04.360
to make sure that everything is happening

00:25:04.360 --> 00:25:06.480
according to plan.

00:25:06.480 --> 00:25:08.480
And if there are any disagreements,

00:25:08.480 --> 00:25:10.800
it's those logs that people fall back on.

00:25:11.880 --> 00:25:18.230
So it's only because there's a standard way of doing this

00:25:18.230 --> 00:25:21.950
that when one machine knocks on another machine's door,

00:25:21.950 --> 00:25:24.270
they know how to talk to each other.

00:25:24.270 --> 00:25:26.830
So there's a lot of vocabularies

00:25:26.830 --> 00:25:28.670
associated with all of this.

00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:31.590
Like there's a lot of very precise things

00:25:31.590 --> 00:25:34.230
that need to be in place in order for one machine

00:25:34.230 --> 00:25:37.190
to know how to do this contract negotiation piece.

00:25:38.710 --> 00:25:43.710
And it's capturing both sides are agreeing to the terms.

00:25:44.950 --> 00:25:47.350
And that's what the logs are capturing as well.

00:25:48.710 --> 00:25:52.470
And importantly,

00:25:52.470 --> 00:25:55.510
the enforcement of all of this

00:25:55.510 --> 00:25:59.350
is up to the implementation of the dataspace.

00:25:59.350 --> 00:26:02.390
So, and that's a function of how much control

00:26:02.390 --> 00:26:04.390
do you want in your dataspace?

00:26:04.390 --> 00:26:07.590
So if you want the data to be highly controlled,

00:26:07.590 --> 00:26:10.350
you want a federated learning solution, right?

00:26:10.350 --> 00:26:12.630
So you're actually just gonna be pushing algorithms

00:26:12.630 --> 00:26:14.750
through this instead of data.

00:26:14.750 --> 00:26:16.190
And the data is just gonna stay

00:26:16.190 --> 00:26:18.990
with each of the providers, right?

00:26:18.990 --> 00:26:21.390
There's all kinds of ways to implement

00:26:21.390 --> 00:26:23.790
the usage controls that people want.

00:26:23.790 --> 00:26:25.670
It could be a trusted research environment,

00:26:25.670 --> 00:26:28.510
a secure container, it could be like all these things

00:26:28.510 --> 00:26:29.870
in the data plane, right?

00:26:29.870 --> 00:26:31.390
Or it could just be an API connection

00:26:31.390 --> 00:26:34.110
where you're gonna push or pull the data.

00:26:34.110 --> 00:26:36.750
So the enforcement of all of this

00:26:36.750 --> 00:26:40.870
is up to the implementation of the individual dataspace

00:26:40.870 --> 00:26:43.350
that is not standardized.

00:26:43.350 --> 00:26:45.230
You just have to implement the standards

00:26:45.230 --> 00:26:53.780
so the machines know how to talk to each other.

00:26:53.780 --> 00:26:56.900
And then the third part of the dataspace protocol

00:26:56.900 --> 00:27:00.300
is just essentially a red light, green light, right?

00:27:00.300 --> 00:27:03.700
So the dataspace connector is the security guard

00:27:03.700 --> 00:27:08.300
who's sitting at the gateway to your repository.

00:27:10.740 --> 00:27:15.740
It's going to have to have a machine operable language

00:27:16.540 --> 00:27:21.460
to understand the request has been put in,

00:27:21.460 --> 00:27:24.700
it's been accepted, green light,

00:27:24.700 --> 00:27:27.500
we've started to send this data across

00:27:27.500 --> 00:27:29.620
and we've completed it.

00:27:29.620 --> 00:27:32.220
And then all of that gets logged.

00:27:32.220 --> 00:27:33.700
So the dataspace protocol

00:27:33.700 --> 00:27:36.460
is about those kinds of three things.

00:27:36.460 --> 00:27:39.820
But again, how you set up the actual implementation

00:27:39.820 --> 00:27:43.620
of usage control, which is really where users

00:27:45.460 --> 00:27:47.220
have a whole lot of variation

00:27:47.220 --> 00:27:48.620
and how much control they need.

00:27:48.620 --> 00:27:52.580
So how much tech do you need to provide users

00:27:52.580 --> 00:27:57.340
with their required amount of assurance?

00:27:57.340 --> 00:28:00.580
So in that RMIT testbed demonstration,

00:28:00.580 --> 00:28:02.140
you could download the data.

00:28:02.140 --> 00:28:04.260
Well, what's keeping you from doing something silly

00:28:04.260 --> 00:28:05.660
with it after you download it?

00:28:07.020 --> 00:28:08.660
Nothing officially, right?

00:28:08.660 --> 00:28:11.860
So, I mean, you've got agreements, you've got onboarding,

00:28:11.860 --> 00:28:14.980
you've got all these sort of legal agreements

00:28:14.980 --> 00:28:16.540
that have come into play,

00:28:16.540 --> 00:28:19.220
but you're not actually able to control the data

00:28:19.220 --> 00:28:20.300
once it's downloaded.

00:28:20.300 --> 00:28:23.700
You might be able to have an encryption thing going on.

00:28:23.700 --> 00:28:26.900
But the point is there's a lot of variation

00:28:26.900 --> 00:28:29.180
in how much usage control you implement.

00:28:30.140 --> 00:28:35.140
But the standards are things you have to implement.

00:28:36.820 --> 00:28:40.140
And again, the nice thing is there's Eclipse

00:28:40.140 --> 00:28:41.460
and there's IDSA.

00:28:41.460 --> 00:28:45.380
And I think even IDSA is moving to the Eclipse connector,

00:28:45.380 --> 00:28:47.180
but the Eclipse connector has a lot of this

00:28:47.180 --> 00:28:50.620
already sorted out for you, which is great.

00:28:50.620 --> 00:28:52.540
It's just if you have a new policy class

00:28:52.540 --> 00:28:53.540
that you need to implement,

00:28:53.540 --> 00:28:56.580
or you have a new kind of thing that you have to work with.

00:29:00.740 --> 00:29:05.740
So now I wanted to just kind of briefly touch on

00:29:05.820 --> 00:29:10.820
how ODRL is sort of used within a dataspace connector,

00:29:12.820 --> 00:29:16.480
just to give you a sense of it.

00:29:16.480 --> 00:29:20.280
So let's say you have the syntax of three components

00:29:20.280 --> 00:29:23.200
in your code, right?

00:29:23.200 --> 00:29:28.200
And on the left side is called an operand.

00:29:29.000 --> 00:29:31.720
And in this case, we're gonna imagine

00:29:31.720 --> 00:29:36.400
that this is the foot and mouth disease use case,

00:29:36.400 --> 00:29:38.560
biosecurity use case, right?

00:29:38.560 --> 00:29:43.560
So this is saying that on the left side,

00:29:44.000 --> 00:29:47.800
on the left side, we need some kind of accreditation

00:29:47.800 --> 00:29:50.400
for the role that the person who's accessing

00:29:50.400 --> 00:29:53.040
wants the data has.

00:29:53.040 --> 00:29:58.040
And that role has to be equal to veterinary officer, right?

00:29:58.120 --> 00:30:01.000
So that's what's written in the ODRL,

00:30:01.000 --> 00:30:04.320
but nothing happens just because it's written there, right?

00:30:04.320 --> 00:30:09.320
The left hand operation triggers the engine

00:30:09.840 --> 00:30:11.640
that is gonna run a function

00:30:11.800 --> 00:30:15.040
that's going to interpret this correctly

00:30:15.040 --> 00:30:18.720
and look to see that, ah yes, in fact,

00:30:19.800 --> 00:30:23.680
that accreditation did come from the trust anchor

00:30:23.680 --> 00:30:25.320
and we've got a token that proves

00:30:25.320 --> 00:30:28.240
that this person is in fact a veterinarian

00:30:28.240 --> 00:30:31.640
and therefore we can push the new code.

00:30:31.640 --> 00:30:33.840
And when you're modifying these things,

00:30:33.840 --> 00:30:36.640
it's easy to modify, okay, and we also need

00:30:36.640 --> 00:30:41.640
a different line of ODRL, right?

00:30:46.160 --> 00:30:49.400
So you can change how you specify these things,

00:30:49.400 --> 00:30:50.480
but then you need to make sure

00:30:50.480 --> 00:30:52.600
that the function written underneath

00:30:52.600 --> 00:30:55.120
actually drives what you want to have happen.

00:30:55.120 --> 00:31:02.080
So that's one reason connectors get tested

00:31:04.000 --> 00:31:05.840
as much as they do and they get certified

00:31:05.880 --> 00:31:10.080
at different levels because it's easy to build a connector.

00:31:10.080 --> 00:31:12.880
It's not easy to build one that works in all cases

00:31:12.880 --> 00:31:14.680
and is highly secure.

00:31:14.680 --> 00:31:17.280
So this piece takes a lot of work.

00:31:17.280 --> 00:31:20.680
What's nice, again, is there are a lot of these things

00:31:20.680 --> 00:31:22.320
that are already pre-baked for us

00:31:22.320 --> 00:31:30.330
that we can pull off the shelf.

00:31:30.330 --> 00:31:34.390
And somebody had a question about federated learning.

00:31:34.390 --> 00:31:37.230
Federated learning can be implemented

00:31:37.230 --> 00:31:41.470
and it's increasingly being implemented within dataspaces,

00:31:41.470 --> 00:31:46.470
but this is where you set up a way of working

00:31:48.550 --> 00:31:53.550
which sends the algorithm to different data sets

00:31:54.550 --> 00:31:56.710
and pulls back the information it needs

00:31:56.710 --> 00:32:01.330
to give you an answer without ever bringing back the raw data.

00:32:02.650 --> 00:32:05.270
And if you're more curious about this,

00:32:05.270 --> 00:32:08.590
I would encourage you to touch base with QCIF.

00:32:08.590 --> 00:32:11.170
They are doing a lot of work in this space.

00:32:12.630 --> 00:32:14.310
There's also a number of other people

00:32:14.310 --> 00:32:15.830
that are doing a lot of work in this space.

00:32:15.830 --> 00:32:20.510
It's a growing use case in dataspaces.

00:32:20.510 --> 00:32:23.030
You're seeing more and more people pushing algorithms

00:32:23.030 --> 00:32:25.910
to data rather than pushing data

00:32:25.910 --> 00:32:29.350
to a central place where the analysis is gonna happen

00:32:29.350 --> 00:32:32.390
or pushing it to just one other provider.

00:32:32.390 --> 00:32:36.910
So this is a growing area of interest.

00:32:36.910 --> 00:32:40.750
It definitely provides probably the strongest control

00:32:40.750 --> 00:32:43.390
over access to your data

00:32:43.390 --> 00:32:45.170
because you're not giving up any of your data.

00:32:45.170 --> 00:32:49.390
You're just agreeing that a mean on these five fields

00:32:49.390 --> 00:32:53.750
is something you're happy for somebody to calculate

00:32:53.750 --> 00:32:55.910
and then export out of your system.

00:32:59.270 --> 00:33:00.350
But again, it's rare.

00:33:00.350 --> 00:33:03.390
There's not a lot of off the shelf solutions.

00:33:03.390 --> 00:33:05.310
QCIF is probably the closest one

00:33:05.310 --> 00:33:07.110
to having off the shelf solutions

00:33:07.110 --> 00:33:10.310
that I know of in Australia.

00:33:10.310 --> 00:33:17.010
There's probably others though.

00:33:17.010 --> 00:33:22.980
So federated learning is essentially,

00:33:24.980 --> 00:33:27.120
it's compute to data, right?

00:33:29.580 --> 00:33:32.760
But again, it can run on the same machinery as a dataspace.

00:33:32.760 --> 00:33:34.980
So there's several proven examples of that.

00:33:34.980 --> 00:33:36.640
And I've already discussed it's not something

00:33:36.640 --> 00:33:40.160
that is in the Eclipse connector set.

00:33:40.160 --> 00:33:46.880
So it's something you'd have to build.

00:33:46.880 --> 00:33:53.280
So another sort of question that came up was,

00:33:53.280 --> 00:33:55.280
couldn't you add a new set of policies

00:33:55.280 --> 00:33:58.220
to an existing dataspace to let a new group

00:33:58.220 --> 00:34:02.420
do something new rather than building one from scratch?

00:34:02.420 --> 00:34:06.440
And so this is played out in Europe in different ways.

00:34:06.440 --> 00:34:10.560
So some European areas do have dataspaces

00:34:10.560 --> 00:34:13.560
with multiple rulebook in place

00:34:14.880 --> 00:34:16.320
across the different actors.

00:34:16.320 --> 00:34:19.760
So if you have a really popular data set

00:34:19.760 --> 00:34:21.800
that all kinds of different people want to use,

00:34:21.800 --> 00:34:26.300
health wants to use it and the environment wants to use it

00:34:26.300 --> 00:34:30.480
and finance wants to use it, whatever it is,

00:34:32.380 --> 00:34:36.040
you might have different rulebook associated

00:34:36.040 --> 00:34:37.880
with each one of those use cases,

00:34:37.880 --> 00:34:39.200
at least to start with.

00:34:40.080 --> 00:34:45.080
But over time and where Europe is trying

00:34:45.200 --> 00:34:48.380
to shift the needle is if you're operating

00:34:48.380 --> 00:34:53.380
within a dataspace, everybody has one overarching rulebook.

00:34:54.780 --> 00:34:59.120
And then there's an opportunity to have modular components

00:34:59.120 --> 00:35:04.120
within that, that specific domains can default to.

00:35:06.120 --> 00:35:10.520
So there's a shift towards rather than having

00:35:11.520 --> 00:35:15.320
one data provider with a whole bunch of rulebook

00:35:15.320 --> 00:35:19.580
in operation to have an overarching rulebook

00:35:19.580 --> 00:35:22.580
and to maybe have modular components within that.

00:35:23.920 --> 00:35:26.280
And I would encourage you to check out

00:35:26.280 --> 00:35:31.280
that YouTube video that's in that space there,

00:35:31.280 --> 00:35:36.280
the mobility dataspace is one of the bigger ones in Europe.

00:35:36.540 --> 00:35:40.300
It has 165 organizations taking part.

00:35:40.520 --> 00:35:43.240
In that dataspace.

00:35:43.240 --> 00:35:50.090
And that includes Mercedes and Volkswagen and BMW

00:35:50.490 --> 00:35:54.870
and insurance companies and meteorological data

00:35:54.870 --> 00:35:58.290
so they can give car owners storm warnings

00:35:58.290 --> 00:36:00.870
that includes road conditions.

00:36:00.870 --> 00:36:04.370
Some jurisdictions have a little map that shows

00:36:04.370 --> 00:36:08.130
where free parking spots are available in town.

00:36:08.130 --> 00:36:10.530
Like all of these use cases have emerged

00:36:10.530 --> 00:36:14.970
because the mobility dataspace has just grown

00:36:14.970 --> 00:36:16.290
and grown and grown.

00:36:16.290 --> 00:36:18.170
You know, the insurance companies are involved

00:36:18.170 --> 00:36:19.650
in the mobility dataspace.

00:36:19.650 --> 00:36:24.650
Like it's become quite a much bigger

00:36:24.890 --> 00:36:27.730
than I think initially envisioned

00:36:28.770 --> 00:36:33.770
because people are becoming increasingly comfortable

00:36:35.010 --> 00:36:36.370
with sharing data.

00:36:36.370 --> 00:36:37.970
So it's a really nice use case.

00:36:37.970 --> 00:36:39.570
I'd encourage you to check out the video

00:36:39.610 --> 00:36:40.450
if you get a chance.

00:36:40.450 --> 00:36:45.450
It's, yeah, they're doing some cool work.

00:36:48.600 --> 00:36:51.320
So now we wanted to cover a little bit

00:36:51.320 --> 00:36:53.440
of how you start a dataspace.

00:36:54.920 --> 00:37:00.960
And again, there's a lot of paths.

00:37:00.960 --> 00:37:02.680
There's even one from Gaia-X.

00:37:02.680 --> 00:37:05.000
So there's a lot of frameworks in place

00:37:05.000 --> 00:37:07.940
that allow you to walk your stakeholders

00:37:07.940 --> 00:37:12.940
through a process that gets them to the dataspace.

00:37:14.720 --> 00:37:17.320
And they don't all align perfectly

00:37:17.320 --> 00:37:20.400
and that can be confusing for people,

00:37:20.400 --> 00:37:24.520
but they all do kind of cover these basics.

00:37:24.520 --> 00:37:28.160
So we're first gonna convene and agree that,

00:37:28.160 --> 00:37:31.960
okay, there's interest in looking into this more deeply.

00:37:31.960 --> 00:37:34.160
Then we're actually gonna make sure

00:37:34.160 --> 00:37:37.120
that all the participants in this dataspace

00:37:37.120 --> 00:37:41.800
can see value in what we're gonna be taking on

00:37:41.800 --> 00:37:44.240
because it's an expensive thing to take on.

00:37:44.240 --> 00:37:48.480
So you build the business case with all of the participants.

00:37:49.440 --> 00:37:51.240
Then you make the decision, yeah, okay,

00:37:51.240 --> 00:37:53.480
we're gonna pull the trigger on this

00:37:53.480 --> 00:37:56.520
because it's a big upfront cost, right?

00:37:56.520 --> 00:38:01.440
It's not a small thing to get involved in a dataspace.

00:38:02.320 --> 00:38:07.320
And along that, there might be, okay,

00:38:08.000 --> 00:38:10.240
well, we just wanna try a use case to start

00:38:10.240 --> 00:38:12.200
or we just might wanna get started.

00:38:12.200 --> 00:38:16.600
So it doesn't always require you pulling the trigger

00:38:16.600 --> 00:38:19.300
on the investment decision completely,

00:38:19.300 --> 00:38:22.240
but it certainly works a lot better when it does happen,

00:38:22.240 --> 00:38:23.080
right?

00:38:25.080 --> 00:38:27.720
And then you work through the governance in the rulebook.

00:38:27.720 --> 00:38:31.280
So that's when you fall back on things like Sitra.

00:38:31.280 --> 00:38:33.680
So the Sitra rulebook is a very nice template

00:38:33.680 --> 00:38:35.840
that you can work through.

00:38:35.840 --> 00:38:39.680
And then you look at how you onboard

00:38:39.720 --> 00:38:43.640
other participants and how you're gonna manage operations.

00:38:43.640 --> 00:38:47.200
And finally you prove the value

00:38:47.200 --> 00:38:50.040
and then you start to scale it.

00:38:50.920 --> 00:38:57.320
So that's generally the shape of,

00:38:57.320 --> 00:38:58.640
and imagine how many meetings

00:38:58.640 --> 00:39:01.140
it's gonna take to get through this, right?

00:39:01.140 --> 00:39:05.400
So the first three are more than three meetings.

00:39:05.400 --> 00:39:07.600
Number four is, oh my goodness,

00:39:07.600 --> 00:39:10.800
it depends on the environment that you're working on,

00:39:10.800 --> 00:39:14.240
but that's gonna include policy people and legal people

00:39:14.240 --> 00:39:16.000
in many instances.

00:39:16.000 --> 00:39:18.680
It is not a short process working

00:39:18.680 --> 00:39:25.620
through that governance rulebook.

00:39:25.620 --> 00:39:30.590
And when you're looking to,

00:39:30.590 --> 00:39:33.790
so there's some cool criteria that are being discussed.

00:39:33.790 --> 00:39:35.530
Thomas is putting them together.

00:39:38.520 --> 00:39:42.740
This doesn't have to proceed those meetings

00:39:42.740 --> 00:39:45.680
of who you get together in the room.

00:39:45.680 --> 00:39:47.680
But essentially if you don't get a yes

00:39:47.720 --> 00:39:49.080
to all three of these,

00:39:50.320 --> 00:39:52.640
you have interest, a business case,

00:39:52.640 --> 00:39:54.400
and you've got justified,

00:39:55.560 --> 00:39:59.760
everybody's feels like the investment is worthy of making.

00:40:02.080 --> 00:40:03.760
Then you probably don't wanna proceed

00:40:03.760 --> 00:40:05.780
with that particular use case, right?

00:40:08.700 --> 00:40:16.180
So we'll come back to that thinking in a minute.

00:40:16.180 --> 00:40:22.250
And so how do you make this idea appealing

00:40:22.250 --> 00:40:24.490
from as quickly as possible?

00:40:25.570 --> 00:40:29.930
So in almost all of the frameworks that you see,

00:40:29.930 --> 00:40:34.370
you really want people to focus on the problem

00:40:34.370 --> 00:40:38.290
and each one of them spelling out

00:40:38.290 --> 00:40:40.790
what the problem is for them,

00:40:40.790 --> 00:40:42.810
not for the sector, not for,

00:40:42.810 --> 00:40:46.450
but to have it captured and written down

00:40:46.450 --> 00:40:50.810
that this is my problem as a data consumer.

00:40:50.810 --> 00:40:53.090
This is my problem as a data provider.

00:40:53.090 --> 00:40:56.090
Oftentimes you'll get a lot of overlap in those things,

00:40:56.090 --> 00:41:01.090
but you wanna hear everyone and you wanna capture it all.

00:41:02.970 --> 00:41:07.530
And then the sovereignty itself

00:41:07.530 --> 00:41:11.130
is a big selling point of a dataspace.

00:41:12.090 --> 00:41:13.650
You're not agreeing to,

00:41:13.650 --> 00:41:16.230
we're all gonna share all our data all the time.

00:41:17.050 --> 00:41:21.010
I might only share my data in an emergency response.

00:41:21.010 --> 00:41:22.990
When foot and mouth disease comes,

00:41:22.990 --> 00:41:27.350
okay, I'll give you the data on where the cows are, right?

00:41:27.350 --> 00:41:29.150
But otherwise I'm not gonna share it.

00:41:29.150 --> 00:41:30.710
That's fine.

00:41:30.710 --> 00:41:35.710
That's up to you as a person who is sharing these data.

00:41:36.590 --> 00:41:41.590
And what we hope to see and what people in Europe have seen

00:41:42.470 --> 00:41:46.250
and the mobility dataspace is a good example of that,

00:41:46.250 --> 00:41:49.270
is as soon as they start seeing the benefit

00:41:49.270 --> 00:41:52.390
of sharing data without the bilateral sharing agreements

00:41:52.390 --> 00:41:54.710
and being able to do it quickly and at scale

00:41:54.710 --> 00:41:57.490
when they want to, with whom they want to,

00:41:57.490 --> 00:42:00.550
they start to see other groups

00:42:00.550 --> 00:42:02.950
that they want to share data with,

00:42:02.950 --> 00:42:06.610
that they ordinarily wouldn't have been sharing data with.

00:42:06.610 --> 00:42:11.610
So the amount of control is very much a big selling point

00:42:15.430 --> 00:42:18.030
to try and sink in early.

00:42:18.950 --> 00:42:23.230
And demos are magic.

00:42:23.230 --> 00:42:27.750
These slideshows are, it's part of the process unfortunately,

00:42:27.750 --> 00:42:32.750
but demos where you're actually able to kick the tires

00:42:34.230 --> 00:42:37.810
are gold and much more convincing

00:42:37.810 --> 00:42:42.810
than me rambling on about things that you should think about.

00:42:42.930 --> 00:42:52.160
So one of the first steps is to form a governance authority

00:42:54.360 --> 00:42:57.960
when you're setting up these organizations.

00:42:57.960 --> 00:43:01.960
And so the governance authority is essentially going to be

00:43:01.960 --> 00:43:06.960
the group of people that are governing the dataspace rulebook.

00:43:08.360 --> 00:43:09.960
So they're gonna take charge of it.

00:43:09.960 --> 00:43:13.360
The rulebook's gonna be formed from the community,

00:43:13.360 --> 00:43:15.820
but the governance authority is the one

00:43:15.860 --> 00:43:18.340
the governance authority is then going to administer

00:43:18.340 --> 00:43:20.140
that rulebook and update it,

00:43:20.140 --> 00:43:22.500
however the community wants to update it.

00:43:22.500 --> 00:43:26.780
So that might be an individual organization.

00:43:26.780 --> 00:43:28.900
It might be a representative body

00:43:28.900 --> 00:43:31.580
with members from each of the organizations.

00:43:31.580 --> 00:43:35.740
It might be funded in a variety of different ways.

00:43:35.740 --> 00:43:40.220
Like there's, it doesn't matter how you get there,

00:43:40.220 --> 00:43:41.580
what it looks like exactly,

00:43:41.580 --> 00:43:44.300
but there needs to be a governance authority in place.

00:43:44.300 --> 00:43:48.580
So there needs to be a group that's gonna be responsible

00:43:48.580 --> 00:43:53.580
for administering and governing the dataspace going forward.

00:43:55.100 --> 00:43:57.500
And it needs to agree on who can join,

00:43:57.500 --> 00:43:59.740
what trust framework are we going to use?

00:43:59.740 --> 00:44:03.260
So what attributes need to be proven,

00:44:03.260 --> 00:44:05.900
what kind of usage controls need to be in place,

00:44:05.900 --> 00:44:07.020
et cetera, et cetera.

00:44:08.980 --> 00:44:11.940
The usage policy framework that you're going to adopt.

00:44:11.940 --> 00:44:13.300
So maybe you don't,

00:44:13.300 --> 00:44:16.940
maybe you can live with the 18 to 23 policy classes

00:44:16.940 --> 00:44:17.780
that are available.

00:44:17.780 --> 00:44:20.580
Maybe you don't need to make any new ones at this time,

00:44:21.780 --> 00:44:23.140
but these are all discussions

00:44:23.140 --> 00:44:25.260
that you have with the stakeholders.

00:44:26.100 --> 00:44:31.100
Legal and contractual and policy things need to be agreed,

00:44:31.940 --> 00:44:34.380
and those things do take time,

00:44:35.380 --> 00:44:37.420
especially the more sensitive the data is

00:44:37.420 --> 00:44:40.260
or the more cross-jurisdictional work you're doing.

00:44:41.180 --> 00:44:43.260
And you need to agree on funding.

00:44:43.260 --> 00:44:46.140
How are we gonna keep the lights on, right?

00:44:49.700 --> 00:44:54.500
And again, the Sitra rulebook is a great place to start.

00:44:55.940 --> 00:44:56.940
It's a template.

00:44:56.940 --> 00:44:59.340
It allows people to work through these things

00:44:59.340 --> 00:45:00.980
at their own pace.

00:45:00.980 --> 00:45:04.580
And if legal isn't a big hurdle for them,

00:45:04.580 --> 00:45:08.100
then they don't have to go into detail on the legal, right?

00:45:08.100 --> 00:45:11.100
But generally, if you're talking

00:45:11.100 --> 00:45:16.460
to the right people, legal is gonna be involved.

00:45:16.460 --> 00:45:19.900
And so governance keeps shared data,

00:45:19.900 --> 00:45:22.500
make sure it's high quality, make sure it's private,

00:45:22.500 --> 00:45:24.020
make sure it's compliant

00:45:24.020 --> 00:45:26.220
with all the rules that have been agreed.

00:45:26.220 --> 00:45:28.100
So that's what the governance authority

00:45:28.100 --> 00:45:30.140
is gonna be charged with.

00:45:30.140 --> 00:45:32.900
It's gonna be charged with making sure

00:45:32.900 --> 00:45:37.100
that data sovereignty and security is respected, right?

00:45:37.100 --> 00:45:39.860
So that might include auditing,

00:45:39.860 --> 00:45:42.820
it might include all kinds of things.

00:45:42.820 --> 00:45:47.340
But it's gonna ensure that the usage control policies

00:45:47.340 --> 00:45:49.140
travel with the data,

00:45:49.140 --> 00:45:55.290
that the level of usage control is in place

00:45:55.290 --> 00:45:56.930
that users require.

00:45:58.610 --> 00:46:01.690
And that the data exchange is trusted,

00:46:01.690 --> 00:46:03.850
it has the right certifications,

00:46:03.850 --> 00:46:06.090
it has the right security levels.

00:46:06.090 --> 00:46:10.290
And it's going to, again, come to consensus

00:46:10.290 --> 00:46:13.210
on how we set these data sharing agreements

00:46:13.210 --> 00:46:20.240
and what usage policies are we going to lean into most.

00:46:20.240 --> 00:46:25.030
So those are what the governance framework

00:46:25.030 --> 00:46:26.710
is going to look at.

00:46:26.710 --> 00:46:29.630
And then within that governance framework,

00:46:29.630 --> 00:46:31.150
there's different building blocks

00:46:31.150 --> 00:46:37.430
that often different participants within the organizations

00:46:37.550 --> 00:46:41.950
that are active will come to agreement on, right?

00:46:41.950 --> 00:46:46.950
So this isn't just usually a handful of people doing this,

00:46:47.470 --> 00:46:52.350
it's a variety of stakeholders in each of the organizations

00:46:52.350 --> 00:46:53.630
that are coming together

00:46:53.630 --> 00:46:56.990
to deliver the full governance framework.

00:46:56.990 --> 00:47:05.290
So you need to think about organizational form and authority.

00:47:06.010 --> 00:47:10.370
So how are you gonna manage decision-making?

00:47:10.370 --> 00:47:13.130
Who has roles and responsibilities for what?

00:47:13.130 --> 00:47:14.850
How are you gonna divide things up?

00:47:14.850 --> 00:47:16.450
Are you gonna have a centralized catalog?

00:47:16.450 --> 00:47:18.050
Are you gonna have a federated catalog?

00:47:18.050 --> 00:47:20.410
Are you going to have a clearing house

00:47:20.410 --> 00:47:23.930
where all of the logs are kept in one place

00:47:23.930 --> 00:47:26.810
and people can visit those clearing houses

00:47:26.810 --> 00:47:28.930
and audit them as needed?

00:47:28.930 --> 00:47:30.350
So all of those,

00:47:31.650 --> 00:47:36.450
and who has a right to do what within the dataspace

00:47:36.450 --> 00:47:38.690
in terms of making changes to the rulebook?

00:47:38.690 --> 00:47:41.930
And so lots and lots of things to think about.

00:47:43.050 --> 00:47:46.570
And how are you gonna onboard a new participant

00:47:46.570 --> 00:47:47.770
to the dataspace?

00:47:49.170 --> 00:47:52.610
How do you figure out what a participant needs to have

00:47:52.610 --> 00:47:55.970
in place before they can become a part of the dataspace?

00:47:57.410 --> 00:48:00.210
And regulatory compliance,

00:48:00.210 --> 00:48:03.810
so this is where depending on the dataspace,

00:48:03.810 --> 00:48:07.770
you can really spend a lot of time, right?

00:48:07.770 --> 00:48:09.970
So Shannon's done some great work

00:48:09.970 --> 00:48:13.490
on working through some of these issues,

00:48:13.490 --> 00:48:16.450
but how do you make sure that everything's aligned

00:48:16.450 --> 00:48:20.130
with existing laws and rules

00:48:20.130 --> 00:48:22.690
associated with that particular dataspace?

00:48:22.690 --> 00:48:26.530
The biosecurity sector has biosecurity law

00:48:26.530 --> 00:48:31.090
that's in place and legislation and policies

00:48:31.090 --> 00:48:33.070
that need to be adhered to.

00:48:35.230 --> 00:48:38.010
And then finally, the contractual framework.

00:48:38.010 --> 00:48:44.160
So is ownership transferred of the data

00:48:44.160 --> 00:48:45.520
when data is transferred?

00:48:45.520 --> 00:48:49.560
Or is ownership strictly kept with the data provider?

00:48:49.560 --> 00:48:52.720
What do those usage rights need to look like?

00:48:52.720 --> 00:48:54.800
Who's liable if things go wrong?

00:48:56.480 --> 00:48:58.640
How do we enforce confidentiality?

00:48:58.640 --> 00:49:00.560
How do we deal with disputes?

00:49:00.560 --> 00:49:05.560
So all of these things are not one hour conversations, right?

00:49:07.480 --> 00:49:10.400
It takes a while to work through these things.

00:49:10.400 --> 00:49:14.320
But if you have people that are really keen

00:49:14.320 --> 00:49:16.240
and already sharing data at scale,

00:49:16.240 --> 00:49:19.880
but they're doing so through bilateral

00:49:19.880 --> 00:49:21.880
data sharing agreements,

00:49:21.880 --> 00:49:25.160
a lot of this is already sort of worked out, right?

00:49:25.160 --> 00:49:32.240
So ideally, you're not trying to force a solution on people,

00:49:33.880 --> 00:49:36.040
especially for these first couple of use cases

00:49:36.040 --> 00:49:36.960
is my thinking.

00:49:36.960 --> 00:49:38.760
You kind of want people that are really keen

00:49:38.760 --> 00:49:39.640
to get this going.

00:49:39.640 --> 00:49:45.620
They just have to get through these hurdles.

00:49:45.620 --> 00:49:48.900
Anyway, I thought rather than me

00:49:49.020 --> 00:49:52.940
just talking at you for two hours,

00:49:52.940 --> 00:49:55.580
that we would break up into groups

00:49:55.580 --> 00:49:59.940
and do a little bit of an exercise

00:49:59.940 --> 00:50:04.660
and give you about 15 minutes to do this.

00:50:05.660 --> 00:50:08.540
But we're gonna break into breakout groups

00:50:08.540 --> 00:50:10.860
and I think we'll just go into three breakout groups

00:50:10.860 --> 00:50:13.060
and randomly assign them.

00:50:14.740 --> 00:50:18.220
And what I want you to think about as a group

00:50:18.220 --> 00:50:21.300
is, okay, foot and mouth disease.

00:50:21.300 --> 00:50:25.780
So this is a terribly infectious disease that infects cattle.

00:50:25.780 --> 00:50:27.180
And as soon as you catch it,

00:50:28.460 --> 00:50:30.660
your export market shuts down.

00:50:30.660 --> 00:50:34.340
You're no longer to export cattle from Australia

00:50:34.340 --> 00:50:36.700
if foot and mouth disease were to arrive here.

00:50:39.940 --> 00:50:44.020
And responders urgently needs to know where the cows are,

00:50:44.020 --> 00:50:49.020
where the incidents of foot and mouth disease are occurring.

00:50:49.300 --> 00:50:53.020
And it needs to happen across the states,

00:50:53.020 --> 00:50:58.410
across producers, across the Commonwealth.

00:50:58.410 --> 00:51:03.410
So how would you set up a preventative dataspace?

00:51:06.500 --> 00:51:08.060
Who would need to be at the table

00:51:08.060 --> 00:51:13.060
in order to gather interest, prove the business case

00:51:13.100 --> 00:51:15.260
and decide to invest?

00:51:15.260 --> 00:51:18.340
So those are the three things I want you to think about.

00:51:18.340 --> 00:51:21.860
So who needs to be at the table

00:51:21.860 --> 00:51:24.620
to make sure that there's interest there?

00:51:25.980 --> 00:51:29.060
How are you gonna prove the business case and with whom?

00:51:30.340 --> 00:51:35.340
And what needs to be in place to go ahead and say,

00:51:35.700 --> 00:51:38.060
you know what, we're gonna make an investment decision.

00:51:38.060 --> 00:51:39.980
It's gonna be $5 million,

00:51:39.980 --> 00:51:46.340
even though foot and mouth disease is not here yet, right?

00:51:46.340 --> 00:51:50.180
So on that note, and what I'd like you to bring back

00:51:50.180 --> 00:51:53.660
is pick somebody in your individual groups

00:51:54.940 --> 00:51:58.820
and bring back one thing

00:51:58.820 --> 00:52:02.540
that your discussion highlights

00:52:02.540 --> 00:52:07.540
in terms of the insight that you think is a key thing

00:52:07.740 --> 00:52:11.420
to getting through these first three gates.

00:52:11.420 --> 00:52:12.820
And I'm gonna show up in a room,

00:52:12.820 --> 00:52:19.620
but I'm gonna stay quiet.

00:52:20.580 --> 00:52:26.800
So take a minute to think about these three things.

00:52:26.800 --> 00:52:31.000
So you wanna get a bunch of stakeholders together,

00:52:31.000 --> 00:52:34.720
gather interest, prove the business case

00:52:34.720 --> 00:52:36.600
and get them to invest.

00:52:36.600 --> 00:52:38.040
Who needs to be in the room?

00:52:39.600 --> 00:52:44.600
How are we gonna decide what's useful for them?

00:52:44.600 --> 00:52:46.000
What's valuable for them?

00:52:47.080 --> 00:52:49.920
And would it be worth the investment?

00:52:49.920 --> 00:52:54.280
And then come back with the one thing

00:52:54.280 --> 00:52:57.880
that you think is the deal breaker here

00:52:57.880 --> 00:52:59.880
or the one insight that's most important

00:52:59.880 --> 00:53:02.440
to get through those three gates.

00:53:02.440 --> 00:53:08.280
So just curious to get your thinking on this.

00:53:08.280 --> 00:53:12.020
So I'm going to,

00:53:12.020 --> 00:53:15.940
you should just be sent to a breakout room here in a second.

00:53:16.940 --> 00:53:57.130
So you should have a thing

00:53:57.130 --> 01:12:13.810
where you can join the breakout room.

01:12:13.810 --> 01:12:14.650
All right.

01:12:15.810 --> 01:12:16.650
Welcome back.

01:12:20.130 --> 01:12:22.770
So I'm curious how these discussions went.

01:12:24.610 --> 01:12:26.970
Maybe we can start with Alison's group.

01:12:26.970 --> 01:12:31.890
Was there somebody who was happy to speak?

01:12:32.890 --> 01:12:35.050
Yeah, I'm happy to do that.

01:12:35.210 --> 01:12:40.130
Rob, did you just want us to talk about the one thing

01:12:40.130 --> 01:12:42.010
or what we had on our list?

01:12:43.730 --> 01:12:45.810
Well, let's start with the one thing

01:12:45.810 --> 01:12:47.410
and then we might circle back.

01:12:48.970 --> 01:12:49.930
All right.

01:12:49.930 --> 01:12:53.810
So our one insight was that the communication

01:12:54.850 --> 01:12:57.890
with our stakeholders was gonna be really important.

01:12:57.890 --> 01:13:00.970
We didn't define what that meant,

01:13:00.970 --> 01:13:04.410
but we were considering that our list of stakeholders

01:13:04.410 --> 01:13:05.810
was extraordinarily long

01:13:07.170 --> 01:13:08.610
and how you actually work

01:13:08.610 --> 01:13:10.490
with such a large number of stakeholders.

01:13:10.490 --> 01:13:13.530
So we decided that, you know,

01:13:13.530 --> 01:13:15.610
you needed to communicate with those stakeholders

01:13:15.610 --> 01:13:17.090
and make sure that you're communicating

01:13:17.090 --> 01:13:18.530
with the decision makers

01:13:18.530 --> 01:13:21.250
within each of those organizations

01:13:21.250 --> 01:13:22.890
as well as, you know,

01:13:22.890 --> 01:13:26.250
really clearly communicating what it's going to cost them

01:13:26.250 --> 01:13:28.410
and the value to them of buying in.

01:13:30.170 --> 01:13:31.810
Is that all right, Alison and Chris?

01:13:31.810 --> 01:13:32.650
Did I cover that?

01:13:32.650 --> 01:13:34.050
Okay.

01:13:34.850 --> 01:13:36.810
It's hard without all the other context

01:13:36.810 --> 01:13:37.930
with the discussion that we had,

01:13:37.930 --> 01:13:43.330
but I think that, you know, sums it up quite nicely.

01:13:43.330 --> 01:13:44.330
Cool.

01:13:44.330 --> 01:13:47.050
So we'll circle back to the more of the discussion

01:13:47.050 --> 01:13:47.890
in a minute,

01:13:47.890 --> 01:13:49.250
but we might go to the next group

01:13:49.250 --> 01:13:51.290
to hear about their one thing.

01:13:52.810 --> 01:14:02.640
So Indika, did you have a spokesperson in your group?

01:14:02.760 --> 01:14:06.160
Eddie, can you, I haven't read it down.

01:14:07.160 --> 01:14:12.560
Yeah, I was a scribe, so I'm happy to report that.

01:14:12.560 --> 01:14:13.560
Thank you.

01:14:16.320 --> 01:14:21.240
So Indika put some notes in the meeting chat,

01:14:21.240 --> 01:14:23.800
which were really interesting as well.

01:14:25.280 --> 01:14:29.480
So other bits that we spoke about,

01:14:30.520 --> 01:14:31.840
we were listing

01:14:32.480 --> 01:14:37.480
the parties that were involved.

01:14:39.160 --> 01:14:40.840
It was a very long list,

01:14:42.320 --> 01:14:45.280
but it was an interesting exercise to go through

01:14:45.280 --> 01:14:50.320
just to see how many people,

01:14:50.320 --> 01:14:54.680
how many areas can be impacted by something like that.

01:14:54.680 --> 01:14:59.680
And we discussed the different type of access

01:15:00.680 --> 01:15:02.680
they would need to data,

01:15:03.520 --> 01:15:07.680
depending on what their interest was.

01:15:09.040 --> 01:15:12.320
I suppose the group with the,

01:15:15.000 --> 01:15:18.040
well, the clearest value,

01:15:18.040 --> 01:15:19.040
there were probably two actually.

01:15:19.040 --> 01:15:24.040
So we talked about local, state and federal government levels

01:15:25.320 --> 01:15:27.960
and primary producers and vets.

01:15:29.920 --> 01:15:31.800
So primary producers and vets,

01:15:31.800 --> 01:15:35.840
we figured have a similar sort of level of interest.

01:15:38.800 --> 01:15:43.800
And we were just discussing the cost of not,

01:15:50.040 --> 01:15:55.040
the cost of no action versus action is like a prevention

01:15:55.880 --> 01:15:57.040
rather than a cure.

01:16:02.980 --> 01:16:06.780
Michael, Indika, anything else that was particularly important?

01:16:06.780 --> 01:16:08.220
So I'm just skimming over the notes,

01:16:08.220 --> 01:16:10.860
trying to pick out the important bits.

01:16:14.960 --> 01:16:18.440
I certainly had an interesting thought on the,

01:16:19.640 --> 01:16:22.280
what actually happens in emergency responses

01:16:22.280 --> 01:16:24.960
is often a bit more chaotic potentially.

01:16:24.960 --> 01:16:27.840
I can only think of like Saturday fire responses

01:16:27.840 --> 01:16:29.120
where a lot of the time,

01:16:29.120 --> 01:16:30.840
we just ended up using Facebook groups

01:16:30.840 --> 01:16:33.920
because the system that was in place

01:16:34.240 --> 01:16:36.240
ended up being too complicated.

01:16:36.240 --> 01:16:41.240
So I think it's almost a case of potentially more business cases

01:16:42.800 --> 01:16:45.280
needs to be put in for the dataspaces.

01:16:45.280 --> 01:16:47.640
It's beyond the immediate emergency,

01:16:47.640 --> 01:16:49.800
just so people get used to using it,

01:16:49.800 --> 01:16:52.400
if anything, as a sort of a matter of course.

01:16:52.400 --> 01:16:56.600
So that was only insight I'd sort of brought up,

01:16:56.600 --> 01:17:02.480
but anything else, Charlie, Indika?

01:17:02.480 --> 01:17:04.680
No, I think that's it for me, thank you.

01:17:04.680 --> 01:17:09.610
Yeah, that's it.

01:17:09.610 --> 01:17:11.330
Okay, thanks.

01:17:11.330 --> 01:17:16.210
And the next group, so Nathan was in that group.

01:17:16.210 --> 01:17:18.010
Do you guys have a speaker?

01:17:18.010 --> 01:17:21.570
That'll be me, it's the scribe and speaker, I guess.

01:17:21.570 --> 01:17:22.410
Yep.

01:17:23.410 --> 01:17:26.250
So I guess the insight we had was that

01:17:27.690 --> 01:17:30.170
you're gonna need to have this set up ahead of time

01:17:31.330 --> 01:17:36.330
because if you try to spin it up

01:17:37.330 --> 01:17:41.370
after you've already got foot and mouth outbreak,

01:17:41.370 --> 01:17:42.690
you just haven't got time.

01:17:42.690 --> 01:17:45.370
And Nathan pointed out that there are

01:17:45.370 --> 01:17:47.650
Standing Emergency Management Committees,

01:17:49.210 --> 01:17:51.130
at least in Victorian State Government,

01:17:51.130 --> 01:17:56.130
which are ready to go and have access to knowledge

01:17:56.770 --> 01:17:59.330
and each of the areas of expertise.

01:18:00.730 --> 01:18:04.290
It could well be one around a foot and mouth outbreak

01:18:04.290 --> 01:18:05.810
as well.

01:18:05.810 --> 01:18:09.170
But if you start organizing meetings with stakeholders

01:18:09.170 --> 01:18:12.930
and things after the outbreak's been detected,

01:18:12.930 --> 01:18:14.850
it's gonna be a nationwide outbreak

01:18:14.850 --> 01:18:17.450
before you finish inviting people to a meeting

01:18:17.450 --> 01:18:23.220
and finding a good time slot that everyone can attend.

01:18:23.220 --> 01:18:27.420
Yep, yes, I definitely agree.

01:18:29.700 --> 01:18:31.700
And that's part of the value proposition

01:18:31.700 --> 01:18:33.540
that you try and sell, right, is that,

01:18:33.820 --> 01:18:37.740
hmm, but where do you think,

01:18:39.020 --> 01:18:41.620
and we'll come back to the other groups on these questions,

01:18:41.620 --> 01:18:44.660
but it sounds like who is at the table is fairly well.

01:18:44.660 --> 01:18:46.180
It's a long list.

01:18:47.620 --> 01:18:51.100
I like the idea that you guys were looking for the heads

01:18:51.100 --> 01:18:55.220
of the key decision makers,

01:18:55.220 --> 01:18:57.100
but also keeping the tent really broad.

01:18:57.100 --> 01:18:59.660
So that's what I was hearing.

01:18:59.660 --> 01:19:04.660
And the idea that communications

01:19:06.380 --> 01:19:08.220
and getting the timing right

01:19:08.220 --> 01:19:11.500
is the two values that I heard most clearly.

01:19:12.740 --> 01:19:14.980
I'm curious where you think it would stall,

01:19:14.980 --> 01:19:17.020
assuming that we did this preventatively

01:19:17.020 --> 01:19:23.270
instead of doing it when the outbreak occurs.

01:19:27.310 --> 01:19:30.150
So where do you think in this process it stalls?

01:19:30.150 --> 01:19:33.470
Does it stall at the first gate of just gathering interest?

01:19:33.470 --> 01:19:38.470
Does it stall with getting the business case sorted out?

01:19:43.190 --> 01:19:46.910
I think often departments are reluctant

01:19:46.910 --> 01:19:49.350
to see the value of preventative things,

01:19:49.350 --> 01:19:51.750
but then there's also the investment piece,

01:19:51.750 --> 01:19:53.830
which is quite substantial.

01:19:53.830 --> 01:19:57.110
So curious to get your thoughts on where you think

01:19:57.110 --> 01:19:59.430
where it would stall in those first three gates,

01:19:59.430 --> 01:20:04.150
given the scale of people that you need to be involved.

01:20:08.420 --> 01:20:11.180
Maybe put you on the spot first, Richard.

01:20:11.180 --> 01:20:14.260
I think you could easily stall at any of these points.

01:20:14.260 --> 01:20:16.980
You may just get every stakeholder,

01:20:16.980 --> 01:20:18.860
especially the industry groups,

01:20:18.860 --> 01:20:22.740
just saying in the typical Australian

01:20:22.740 --> 01:20:25.180
and particularly Northern Territorian way,

01:20:25.180 --> 01:20:26.340
she'll be right, mate.

01:20:27.380 --> 01:20:28.220
Yep.

01:20:31.660 --> 01:20:35.100
I think the value and the business case

01:20:35.100 --> 01:20:40.100
is absolutely obvious.

01:20:40.660 --> 01:20:44.780
The cost of an unmanaged foot and mouth outbreak

01:20:44.780 --> 01:20:46.540
across Australia would be measured

01:20:46.540 --> 01:20:52.490
in the billions and billions of dollars.

01:20:52.490 --> 01:20:54.130
Yep, exactly.

01:20:54.130 --> 01:20:59.130
I think it would stall on every other front.

01:21:00.170 --> 01:21:02.050
People aren't willing to put the time in,

01:21:02.050 --> 01:21:03.210
aren't willing to put the money in,

01:21:03.210 --> 01:21:05.090
aren't willing to put the effort in,

01:21:05.090 --> 01:21:07.610
aren't willing to bring data to the table.

01:21:07.610 --> 01:21:10.570
I just think there would be so many impediments

01:21:10.570 --> 01:21:14.890
that it's just chances of getting something

01:21:14.890 --> 01:21:16.170
over the line ahead of time

01:21:16.170 --> 01:21:20.210
that would be very small, I reckon.

01:21:20.210 --> 01:21:24.330
Yeah, I think when we talk to people

01:21:24.330 --> 01:21:25.650
who do surveillance for a living,

01:21:25.650 --> 01:21:28.170
they're very keen on this outcome.

01:21:28.170 --> 01:21:30.370
But with the PHA use case,

01:21:33.450 --> 01:21:35.290
Shannon and I very much felt like

01:21:35.290 --> 01:21:38.290
we weren't dealing with stakeholders

01:21:38.290 --> 01:21:40.010
that were high enough up the food chain

01:21:40.010 --> 01:21:41.770
to really push things along,

01:21:41.770 --> 01:21:43.410
and we're gonna end up with a use case

01:21:43.410 --> 01:21:46.930
that was sort of maybe a shiny little thing,

01:21:46.930 --> 01:21:49.250
but not one that anyone was necessarily

01:21:49.250 --> 01:21:51.170
gonna pay a whole lot of attention to.

01:21:52.850 --> 01:21:55.770
So I do think for a use case like this,

01:21:55.770 --> 01:21:57.250
you would need the big end of town

01:21:57.250 --> 01:21:59.250
to be backing you very solidly

01:22:00.250 --> 01:22:03.690
to get the rest of folks to buy in.

01:22:04.810 --> 01:22:06.970
A couple of really big stakeholders

01:22:06.970 --> 01:22:11.370
was my sense with this particular use case.

01:22:11.370 --> 01:22:12.210
Anyway.

01:22:12.210 --> 01:22:13.890
Nice one's got his hand up.

01:22:15.090 --> 01:22:15.930
Please.

01:22:17.450 --> 01:22:19.610
With this particular scenario,

01:22:19.610 --> 01:22:24.610
you go to the top, so it's DAFF,

01:22:25.450 --> 01:22:29.010
because this is all under policy and regulation.

01:22:30.570 --> 01:22:33.810
But Michael's suggestion before is a good one.

01:22:37.010 --> 01:22:38.810
Federal and state government

01:22:38.810 --> 01:22:42.250
have mock-up scenarios all the time.

01:22:43.130 --> 01:22:47.610
So what you could do is

01:22:50.250 --> 01:22:52.330
do some outreach,

01:22:52.330 --> 01:22:56.450
say DAFF and say MLA,

01:22:57.730 --> 01:23:01.450
because they would do these scenario models anyway,

01:23:01.450 --> 01:23:05.050
and see if it could be coupled up as a piece of work

01:23:05.050 --> 01:23:07.410
to demonstrate how it could support.

01:23:07.410 --> 01:23:09.290
So you don't even need real data.

01:23:10.450 --> 01:23:12.250
You could actually have a mock-up

01:23:14.010 --> 01:23:17.050
and almost a little bit like,

01:23:17.050 --> 01:23:18.930
what are those sessions where you put all the data up

01:23:18.930 --> 01:23:20.810
and see what you can do sort of thing.

01:23:22.530 --> 01:23:25.370
Something like that that could be run in parallel

01:23:27.290 --> 01:23:31.210
and think about the needs of the process,

01:23:31.210 --> 01:23:35.290
because I'll have a process defined that they must enforce.

01:23:35.290 --> 01:23:37.730
They'll bring out the guidelines,

01:23:37.730 --> 01:23:40.530
what we have to do in an emergency.

01:23:40.530 --> 01:23:43.770
So it'd be working through that in parallel

01:23:44.610 --> 01:23:47.850
to say, oh, where would some of these fit in

01:23:47.850 --> 01:23:50.530
or help support or improve.

01:23:50.530 --> 01:23:53.450
At the end of the day, it's improve the response.

01:23:55.090 --> 01:23:56.730
Because I think that's what we talked about,

01:23:56.730 --> 01:24:00.410
wasn't it, Richard, about all of a sudden

01:24:00.410 --> 01:24:05.410
if something blows out to six, nine, 10, 12 months,

01:24:06.890 --> 01:24:09.770
rather than it can contain it within a few,

01:24:09.770 --> 01:24:12.890
less than a month, the financial benefits are massive.

01:24:12.890 --> 01:24:18.690
So it's about the responsiveness to the need.

01:24:18.690 --> 01:24:20.330
Yeah.

01:24:20.330 --> 01:24:21.450
Yeah, I absolutely agree with that.

01:24:21.450 --> 01:24:23.090
And I think the folks at CEBRA

01:24:23.090 --> 01:24:24.170
at the University of Melbourne

01:24:24.170 --> 01:24:25.770
would agree with that as well.

01:24:29.370 --> 01:24:32.250
I think it helps if you're able to get some of the RDCs

01:24:32.250 --> 01:24:34.810
or some of the players that are able to invest in it

01:24:34.810 --> 01:24:37.610
a little bit more to make it more tangible.

01:24:39.330 --> 01:24:43.170
But yeah, that would be a useful approach, I think.

01:24:47.420 --> 01:24:50.740
Let's see, we might circle back to Brigitte

01:24:50.740 --> 01:24:55.500
and highlight where you think your scenario might stall

01:24:55.500 --> 01:24:57.540
and if there was other details

01:24:57.540 --> 01:25:03.890
that came out of your discussion that you wanna share.

01:25:03.890 --> 01:25:05.330
Not sure I've got anything to add

01:25:05.330 --> 01:25:08.010
on top of what's already just been said.

01:25:08.010 --> 01:25:09.970
The main thing we touched on

01:25:09.970 --> 01:25:13.570
was the fact that there were so many potential stakeholders

01:25:13.570 --> 01:25:18.570
and some will be data consumers or responders,

01:25:19.090 --> 01:25:21.090
some might be data providers.

01:25:21.090 --> 01:25:23.370
So trying to sort through how you engage

01:25:23.370 --> 01:25:27.010
all these people organisations would be challenging.

01:25:28.210 --> 01:25:31.210
But yeah, probably don't have much more to add

01:25:31.210 --> 01:25:36.050
unless Chris and Alison want me to point out anything.

01:25:36.050 --> 01:25:38.450
Oh, actually one of the things we did say is that

01:25:40.050 --> 01:25:42.650
because we thought that the value proposition

01:25:42.650 --> 01:25:46.690
was about the prevention of bigger costs down the track,

01:25:46.690 --> 01:25:48.650
which has already been noted.

01:25:49.410 --> 01:25:54.370
And so we thought that one of the things that you'd need to do

01:25:54.370 --> 01:25:56.890
to make it demonstrate why it's worth investing

01:25:56.890 --> 01:26:01.730
is to do some scenarios, economic scenarios to go again.

01:26:01.730 --> 01:26:04.570
This is what happens if you don't do this preparedness

01:26:04.570 --> 01:26:06.330
exercise that costs five million,

01:26:06.330 --> 01:26:09.570
it will end up costing you five billion or something.

01:26:09.570 --> 01:26:11.730
So that we thought that scenarios

01:26:11.730 --> 01:26:15.280
would be useful there too.

01:26:15.280 --> 01:26:16.240
Yeah, that makes sense.

01:26:16.240 --> 01:26:19.160
And CEBRA has done some amazing work in that space.

01:26:19.160 --> 01:26:21.520
So they've calculated the cost of

01:26:22.840 --> 01:26:26.040
what the biosecurity sector in its current state,

01:26:26.040 --> 01:26:28.320
what the savings that it provides is

01:26:28.320 --> 01:26:30.520
and it's in the billions of dollars.

01:26:30.520 --> 01:26:32.800
And they also have a workflow that allows you

01:26:32.800 --> 01:26:35.760
to estimate the cost of different scenarios.

01:26:35.760 --> 01:26:39.080
So actually to model that for specific incursions.

01:26:40.840 --> 01:26:44.600
So yeah, all of that thinking is definitely in play

01:26:44.600 --> 01:26:46.640
on the side of the fence that would love

01:26:46.640 --> 01:26:48.640
to see a dataspace, but doesn't have

01:26:48.640 --> 01:26:51.560
the backing to pull it off.

