01:27:41.480 --> 01:27:46.480
And one of the reasons that dataspaces often start

01:27:47.440 --> 01:27:50.960
with a smaller use case is because taking on

01:27:50.960 --> 01:27:55.960
the entire biosecurity sector in one, it is too much, right?

01:27:56.880 --> 01:28:00.420
So you identify a specific use case where,

01:28:01.360 --> 01:28:04.120
so if we go back to Varroa mites and the bees

01:28:04.120 --> 01:28:06.560
that had a little parasite living on them

01:28:06.560 --> 01:28:09.640
that infected New South Wales honey or something,

01:28:09.640 --> 01:28:14.040
and you let that data play out,

01:28:14.040 --> 01:28:16.920
that's one way that people build use cases

01:28:16.920 --> 01:28:20.240
that then can scale later, and you don't have to start

01:28:20.240 --> 01:28:22.080
with the whole kettle of fish.

01:28:23.220 --> 01:28:27.320
Often that's the only thing you have resource to pull off,

01:28:27.320 --> 01:28:32.320
but in an ideal world, you do have that top-end buy-in

01:28:32.440 --> 01:28:35.120
from the real movers and shakers,

01:28:35.120 --> 01:28:39.040
and you have that broad scale engagement across everyone

01:28:39.040 --> 01:28:42.540
to build the rulebook in the first instance.

01:28:42.540 --> 01:28:44.360
That just rarely happens.

01:28:44.360 --> 01:28:48.500
But if you come across that gold nugget of a use case

01:28:48.500 --> 01:28:51.520
or of a group of stakeholders who wanna build a dataspace,

01:28:51.520 --> 01:28:54.140
then you're really away, and you're gonna scale

01:28:54.140 --> 01:28:58.180
and build much faster than if you start with a use case.

01:28:58.180 --> 01:29:01.040
But often in Europe, they start with a use case,

01:29:01.040 --> 01:29:03.120
so they start with a much smaller scope

01:29:03.120 --> 01:29:05.000
and number of actors.

01:29:05.820 --> 01:29:06.920
And just demonstrate value.

